{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_013HARf5qyDQXgrsFEjZ2CKq","type":"tool_result","content":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The bibliography has three entries (Feige upper and lower bounds, Kiwi et al. on hyperbolic random graphs). The Feige and Kiwi citations are adequate. Three places in the draft need targeted retrieval. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Aleliunas et al. random-walk cover time bound / universal traversal sequences\n- Lovász survey on random walks on graphs\n- Commute-time identity, effective resistance and Matthews-type cover time bounds\n- Ridge regression (Hoerl and Kennard)\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. related-work-worst-case-bounds (high, comparator)\n- **Section:** Related Work (also the Introduction)\n- **Anchor:** \"The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al. follows by summing commute times ... Lovász's survey on random walks on graphs\"\n- **Reason:** The 2m(n-1) bound is the headline comparator. It is attributed only by author name, with no `\\cite`. The Lovász survey is also named without a citation. A reviewer would expect both to be cited directly.\n- **Search topics:** Aleliunas–Karp–Lipton–Lovász–Rackoff, \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\"; Lovász, \"Random walks on graphs: a survey\".\n\n### 2. discussion-resistance-matthews-features (medium, positioning)\n- **Section:** Discussion and Limitations (the Conclusion repeats the point)\n- **Anchor:** \"resistance-based quantities motivated by the commute-time identity and Matthews-type bounds\"\n- **Reason:** The paper's main proposed next step, richer resistance-based features, rests on the commute-time identity and Matthews bounds. Neither has any support in the draft or the bibliography.\n- **Search topics:** Chandra et al., electrical resistance and commute/cover times; Matthews bounds on covering times via maximum hitting time.\n\n### 3. method-ridge-regression-background (low, background)\n- **Section:** Related Work (\"Statistical prediction with cheap features\")\n- **Anchor:** \"Predicting a graph functional from low-dimensional summaries is a standard use of regression\"\n- **Reason:** The paper claims no novelty for the model class, so a single classic ridge reference suffices.\n- **Search topics:** Hoerl and Kennard, \"Ridge regression: biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\".\n\n## Deliberately not targeted\n- Quantitative claims, the evaluation protocol and the closed-form cover times are grounded in `paper_data.json` and the repository scripts.\n- Broader literatures were skipped (cover times of random regular or Erdős–Rényi graphs, learned graph-property prediction). Covering them would reshape the related-work story rather than fill a thin spot.\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Central topic\nRidge-regression prediction of start-averaged cover time on small connected graphs (6 ≤ n ≤ 30), compared against the generic 2m(n-1) worst-case bound. This plan only fills citation gaps exposed by the current draft.\n\n## Existing bibliography (not re-queried)\n- Feige, tight upper bound on cover time\n- Feige, tight lower bound on cover time\n- Kiwi et al., cover and hitting times of hyperbolic random graphs\n\nThe gap artifact judges these adequate, so they are excluded.\n\n## Queries (5)\n\n1. **Aleliunas et al., \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\"** (`baseline_comparator`). This is the source of the 2m(n-1) bound, the headline comparator. It is attributed by author name only, in the Introduction and in Related Work (worst-case bounds). It has priority high.\n2. **Lovász, \"Random walks on graphs: a survey\"** (`baseline_comparator`). The Related Work worst-case-bounds paragraph names this survey for hitting-time and commute-time machinery without a citation. It has priority high and shares the same paragraph as query 1.\n3. **Chandra et al., \"The electrical resistance of a graph captures its commute and cover times\"** (`core_method`). Discussion (\"Feature attribution and ceiling\") and the Conclusion propose resistance-based features, motivated by the commute-time identity, as the next step. Nothing currently supports that. It has priority medium.\n4. **Matthews bounds on covering times via maximum hitting time** (`core_method`). It supports the \"Matthews-type bounds\" claim in the same passages and the unscored Matthews-style candidate in the supplementary model-selection note. It has priority medium.\n5. **Hoerl and Kennard, \"Ridge regression: biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\"** (`core_method`). Related Work (\"Statistical prediction with cheap features\") and the Method's ridge estimator are uncited. One classic reference is enough because the paper claims no novelty for the model class. It has priority low.\n\n## Deliberately excluded\n- Cover times of random regular or Erdős–Rényi graphs, and learned graph-property prediction. The gap artifact skips these because they would reshape the related-work story rather than fill a thin spot.\n- Re-retrieval of the Feige and Kiwi references.\n- Quantitative claims, the evaluation protocol and the closed-form cover times. These are grounded in the run's data files and scripts, not in literature.\n@Article{Feige1995ATL,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {433-438},\n title = {A Tight Lower Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs},\n volume = {6},\n year = {1995}\n}\n\n@Article{Feige1995ATU,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {51-54},\n title = {A Tight Upper Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs},\n volume = {6},\n year = {1995}\n}\n\n@Article{Kiwi2022CoverAH,\n author = {M. Kiwi and Markus Schepers and John A. Sylvester},\n booktitle = {International Workshop and International Workshop on Approximation, Randomization, and Combinatorial Optimization. Algorithms and Techniques},\n journal = {Random Structures & Algorithms},\n pages = {915 - 978},\n title = {Cover and hitting times of hyperbolic random graphs},\n volume = {65},\n year = {2022}\n}\n[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 5 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/5 | Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/3 (3 left) | candidates 0/1 | query 1/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [####--------] | refs 1/3 (2 left) | candidates 1/1 | query 1/5\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 2/5 | Lovász Random walks on graphs: a survey\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [####--------] | refs 1/3 (2 left) | candidates 0/25 | query 2/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [########----] | refs 2/3 (1 left) | candidates 1/25 | query 2/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [############] | refs 3/3 (0 left) | candidates 2/25 | query 2/5\n@Article{Feige1995ATL,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {433-438},\n{\n \"campaign_results_dir\": \"/home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171535Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171558/investigation\",\n \"count\": 3,\n \"generated_at\": \"2026-10-27T17:36:05.683970+00:00\",\n \"queries\": [\n  \"random walks universal traversal sequences complexity of maze problems cover time bound 2m(n-1)\",\n  \"Feige tight upper bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\",\n  \"Feige tight lower bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\",\n  \"Lov\\u00e1sz random walks on graphs: a survey hitting times commute times cover time\",\n  \"Matthews bounds for cover times of Markov chains maximum hitting time harmonic number\",\n  \"electrical resistance of a graph captures its commute and cover times\",\n  \"maximum hitting time for random walks on graphs lollipop extremal\",\n  \"cover time of random regular graphs and sparse Erd\\u0151s\\u2013R\\u00e9nyi random graphs\",\n  \"ridge regression biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\"\n ],\n \"reference_query_plan\": {\n  \"architectural_shifts\": [\n   \"Shift from worst-case combinatorial bound (2m(n-1)) to a calibrated log-linear predictor on cheap statistics\",\n   \"Proposed shift from degree/diameter statistics to spectral/electrical (effective-resistance) features; untested in final evidence\"\n  ],\n  \"benchmarks\": [\n   \"Programmatically generated connected graphs, n in [6,30], 12 families: path, cycle, star, complete, grid, complete bipartite, barbell, lollipop, binary tree, random tree, Erdos-Renyi, random regular\",\n   \"Task-internal Monte Carlo ground truth (median relative MC SE about 2.9%); no external dataset\"\n  ],\n  \"central_topic\": \"Predicting the start-averaged expected cover time of simple random walks on small connected graphs (6<=n<=30) from cheap graph statistics and effective-resistance (Matthews-type) features, compared against generic worst-case bounds such as 2m(n-1), with error analyzed by graph family under Monte Carlo uncertainty.\",\n  \"comparators\": [\n   \"Generic worst-case cover time bound 2m(n-1) (Aleliunas et al. 1979)\",\n   \"Feige's tight upper/lower bounds on cover time (n^3-scale, 4/27 n^3)\",\n   \"Matthews bound via maximum hitting time times harmonic number\",\n   \"Resistance-based commute/cover time bounds (Chandra et al.)\",\n   \"Constant (family-agnostic) predictor and label-shuffled predictor as sanity controls\"\n  ],\n  \"core_methods\": [\n   \"Log-linear (ridge) regression of log cover time on log n, log m, log mean degree, log max degree, log diameter (the evaluated baseline)\",\n   \"Matthews-type predictor: m*R_max*H_{n-1} using effective resistances from the Laplacian pseudo-inverse / hitting-time linear solves (proposed idea; implemented but not scored in the final evidence)\",\n   \"Monte Carlo estimation of start-averaged cover time with standard errors\",\n   \"Leave-one-family-out evaluation with family-macro log error\"\n  ],\n  \"forbidden_query_patterns\": [\n   \"generic 'optimization', 'benchmarking', 'scientific method', or paper-writing phrases\",\n   \"queries about run chronology, attempts, or search history\",\n   \"queries targeting the original paper answering this research question or any page discussing it (task prohibits)\",\n   \"deep-learning/GNN cover-time prediction queries not supported by artifacts\",\n   \"external dataset names (none used)\"\n  ],\n  \"queries\": [\n   {\n    \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n    \"query\": \"random walks universal traversal sequences complexity of maze problems cover time bound 2m(n-1)\",\n    \"rationale\": \"Source of the generic 2m(n-1) bound that is the primary comparator (bound macro log MAE 1.754).\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n    \"query\": \"Feige tight upper bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\",\n    \"rationale\": \"Tight n^3-scale worst-case bound; starter literature and a looseness baseline.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n    \"query\": \"Feige tight lower bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\",\n    \"rationale\": \"Companion lower bound, needed to frame the range within which small-graph cover times can fall.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"category\": \"core_method\",\n    \"query\": \"Lov\\u00e1sz random walks on graphs: a survey hitting times commute times cover time\",\n    \"rationale\": \"Starter survey; standard reference for hitting/commute-time identities used in the resistance features.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"category\": \"core_method\",\n    \"query\": \"Matthews bounds for cover times of Markov chains maximum hitting time harmonic number\",\n    \"rationale\": \"Basis of the Matthews-type offset m*R_max*H_{n-1} in the proposed predictor.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"category\": \"core_method\",\n    \"query\": \"electrical resistance of a graph captures its commute and cover times\",\n    \"rationale\": \"Justifies effective-resistance features (Laplacian pseudo-inverse) and resistance-based cover time bounds.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"category\": \"result_driven_theme\",\n    \"query\": \"maximum hitting time for random walks on graphs lollipop extremal\",\n    \"rationale\": \"Lollipop/barbell are test families and the expected worst case for cheap-statistic predictors; extremal-graph theory explains family-dependent error.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"category\": \"benchmark_context\",\n    \"query\": \"cover time of random regular graphs and sparse Erd\\u0151s\\u2013R\\u00e9nyi random graphs\",\n    \"rationale\": \"Random regular and Erd\\u0151s\\u2013R\\u00e9nyi are benchmark families; asymptotic cover times give context for where simple predictors should be reliable.\"\n   },\n   {\n    \"category\": \"baseline_comparator\",\n    \"query\": \"ridge regression biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\",\n    \"rationale\": \"The evaluated baseline is ridge regression on standardized log features.\"\n   }\n  ],\n  \"result_driven_themes\": [\n   \"Final evaluation of the ridge baseline: family-macro log MAE 0.181 versus 1.754 for the 2m(n-1) bound, 0.870 for a constant predictor and 1.423 for shuffled labels, i.e. the generic bound is loose by a large multiplicative factor on small graphs\",\n   \"Error depend\nsame","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bd783da-1739-49a5-a1ff-6fbbac76c044","uuid":"b1575a82-041b-43f7-ab13-6470a01aca3e","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:48:58.658Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# Draft reference gap analysis\n\n**Status:** ready. The bibliography has three entries (Feige upper and lower bounds, Kiwi et al. on hyperbolic random graphs). The Feige and Kiwi citations are adequate. Three places in the draft need targeted retrieval. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Missing literatures\n- Aleliunas et al. random-walk cover time bound / universal traversal sequences\n- Lovász survey on random walks on graphs\n- Commute-time identity, effective resistance and Matthews-type cover time bounds\n- Ridge regression (Hoerl and Kennard)\n\n## Rewrite targets\n\n### 1. related-work-worst-case-bounds (high, comparator)\n- **Section:** Related Work (also the Introduction)\n- **Anchor:** \"The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al. follows by summing commute times ... Lovász's survey on random walks on graphs\"\n- **Reason:** The 2m(n-1) bound is the headline comparator. It is attributed only by author name, with no `\\cite`. The Lovász survey is also named without a citation. A reviewer would expect both to be cited directly.\n- **Search topics:** Aleliunas–Karp–Lipton–Lovász–Rackoff, \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\"; Lovász, \"Random walks on graphs: a survey\".\n\n### 2. discussion-resistance-matthews-features (medium, positioning)\n- **Section:** Discussion and Limitations (the Conclusion repeats the point)\n- **Anchor:** \"resistance-based quantities motivated by the commute-time identity and Matthews-type bounds\"\n- **Reason:** The paper's main proposed next step, richer resistance-based features, rests on the commute-time identity and Matthews bounds. Neither has any support in the draft or the bibliography.\n- **Search topics:** Chandra et al., electrical resistance and commute/cover times; Matthews bounds on covering times via maximum hitting time.\n\n### 3. method-ridge-regression-background (low, background)\n- **Section:** Related Work (\"Statistical prediction with cheap features\")\n- **Anchor:** \"Predicting a graph functional from low-dimensional summaries is a standard use of regression\"\n- **Reason:** The paper claims no novelty for the model class, so a single classic ridge reference suffices.\n- **Search topics:** Hoerl and Kennard, \"Ridge regression: biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\".\n\n## Deliberately not targeted\n- Quantitative claims, the evaluation protocol and the closed-form cover times are grounded in `paper_data.json` and the repository scripts.\n- Broader literatures were skipped (cover times of random regular or Erdős–Rényi graphs, learned graph-property prediction). Covering them would reshape the related-work story rather than fill a thin spot.\n# Post-draft reference query plan\n\n**Status:** ready. No retrieval was run in this stage.\n\n## Central topic\nRidge-regression prediction of start-averaged cover time on small connected graphs (6 ≤ n ≤ 30), compared against the generic 2m(n-1) worst-case bound. This plan only fills citation gaps exposed by the current draft.\n\n## Existing bibliography (not re-queried)\n- Feige, tight upper bound on cover time\n- Feige, tight lower bound on cover time\n- Kiwi et al., cover and hitting times of hyperbolic random graphs\n\nThe gap artifact judges these adequate, so they are excluded.\n\n## Queries (5)\n\n1. **Aleliunas et al., \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\"** (`baseline_comparator`). This is the source of the 2m(n-1) bound, the headline comparator. It is attributed by author name only, in the Introduction and in Related Work (worst-case bounds). It has priority high.\n2. **Lovász, \"Random walks on graphs: a survey\"** (`baseline_comparator`). The Related Work worst-case-bounds paragraph names this survey for hitting-time and commute-time machinery without a citation. It has priority high and shares the same paragraph as query 1.\n3. **Chandra et al., \"The electrical resistance of a graph captures its commute and cover times\"** (`core_method`). Discussion (\"Feature attribution and ceiling\") and the Conclusion propose resistance-based features, motivated by the commute-time identity, as the next step. Nothing currently supports that. It has priority medium.\n4. **Matthews bounds on covering times via maximum hitting time** (`core_method`). It supports the \"Matthews-type bounds\" claim in the same passages and the unscored Matthews-style candidate in the supplementary model-selection note. It has priority medium.\n5. **Hoerl and Kennard, \"Ridge regression: biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\"** (`core_method`). Related Work (\"Statistical prediction with cheap features\") and the Method's ridge estimator are uncited. One classic reference is enough because the paper claims no novelty for the model class. It has priority low.\n\n## Deliberately excluded\n- Cover times of random regular or Erdős–Rényi graphs, and learned graph-property prediction. The gap artifact skips these because they would reshape the related-work story rather than fill a thin spot.\n- Re-retrieval of the Feige and Kiwi references.\n- Quantitative claims, the evaluation protocol and the closed-form cover times. These are grounded in the run's data files and scripts, not in literature.\n@Article{Feige1995ATL,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {433-438},\n title = {A Tight Lower Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs},\n volume = {6},\n year = {1995}\n}\n\n@Article{Feige1995ATU,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {51-54},\n title = {A Tight Upper Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs},\n volume = {6},\n year = {1995}\n}\n\n@Article{Kiwi2022CoverAH,\n author = {M. Kiwi and Markus Schepers and John A. Sylvester},\n booktitle = {International Workshop and International Workshop on Approximation, Randomization, and Combinatorial Optimization. Algorithms and Techniques},\n journal = {Random Structures & Algorithms},\n pages = {915 - 978},\n title = {Cover and hitting times of hyperbolic random graphs},\n volume = {65},\n year = {2022}\n}\n[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 5 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/5 | Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/3 (3 left) | candidates 0/1 | query 1/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [####--------] | refs 1/3 (2 left) | candidates 1/1 | query 1/5\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 2/5 | Lovász Random walks on graphs: a survey\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [####--------] | refs 1/3 (2 left) | candidates 0/25 | query 2/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [########----] | refs 2/3 (1 left) | candidates 1/25 | query 2/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [############] | refs 3/3 (0 left) | candidates 2/25 | query 2/5\n@Article{Feige1995ATL,\n author = {U. Feige},\n booktitle = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n journal = {Random Struct. Algorithms},\n pages = {433-438},\n{\n \"campaign_results_dir\": \"/home/user/orca/projects/researchworld-eval/.project/runs/agentic-scientist-s00-20261027T171535Z/work/results/s00/claude/20261027_171558/investigation\",\n \"count\": 3,\n \"generated_at\": \"2026-10-27T17:36:05.683970+00:00\",\n \"queries\": [\n  \"random walks universal traversal sequences complexity of maze problems cover time bound 2m(n-1)\",\n  \"Feige tight upper bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\",\n  \"Feige tight lower bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\",\n  \"Lov\\u00e1sz random walks on graphs: a survey hitting times commute times cover time\",\n  \"Matthews bounds for cover times of Markov chains maximum hitting time harmonic number\",\n  \"electrical resistance of a graph captures its commute and cover times\",\n  \"maximum hitting time for random walks on graphs lollipop extremal\",\n  \"cover time of random regular graphs and sparse Erd\\u0151s\\u2013R\\u00e9nyi random graphs\",\n  \"ridge regression biased estimation for nonorthogonal problems\"\n ],\n \"reference_query_plan