{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01K1D3WCEPHyNG4VanMtgmuJ","type":"tool_result","content":"[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 5 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/5 | Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/3 (3 left) | candidates 0/1 | query 1/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [####--------] | refs 1/3 (2 left) | candidates 1/1 | query 1/5\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 2/5 | Lovász Random walks on graphs: a survey\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [####--------] | refs 1/3 (2 left) | candidates 0/25 | query 2/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [########----] | refs 2/3 (1 left) | candidates 1/25 | query 2/5\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [############] | refs 3/3 (0 left) | candidates 2/25 | query 2/5\n[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 9 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/9 | random walks universal traversal sequences complexity of maze problems cover time bound 2m(n-1)\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/3 (3 left) | candidates 0/0 | query 1/9\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 2/9 | Feige tight upper bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [------------] | refs 0/3 (3 left) | candidates 0/2 | query 2/9\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [####--------] | refs 1/3 (2 left) | candidates 1/2 | query 2/9\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [########----] | refs 2/3 (1 left) | candidates 2/2 | query 2/9\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 3/9 | Feige tight lower bound on the cover time for random walks on graphs\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [########----] | refs 2/3 (1 left) | candidates 0/0 | query 3/9\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 4/9 | Lovász random walks on graphs: a survey hitting times commute times cover time\n[semantic_scholar] search complete | [########----] | refs 2/3 (1 left) | candidates 0/1 | query 4/9\n[semantic_scholar] fetching | [############] | refs 3/3 (0 left) | candidates 1/1 | query 4/9\n[paper.sh] attempting scholarly fallback: OpenAlex fallback did not produce references\n[paper.sh] downstream fallbacks unavailable; using Semantic Scholar references\n[('ref.s2.fd5abaa009c1bf1ffbf92ef33e4c556560009774', 'A Tight Lower Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs', None), ('ref.s2.3fd43be5cd805edb3b4f49e2f250a1cead558c43', 'A Tight Upper Bound on the Cover Time for Random Walks on Graphs', None), ('ref.s2.72156e55f0735a30720b4a46e19a142deb951753', 'Cover and hitting times of hyperbolic random graphs', None)]\ndict_keys(['campaign_results_dir', 'count', 'generated_at', 'queries', 'reference_query_plan', 'reference_query_plans', 'references', 'results_dir'])\ngenerate-concept-figure\nmemory-log\nretrieve-references\nscientific-evidence\nvalidate-paper\nwrite-paper-standalone\n39:The cover time of a connected undirected graph is the expected number of steps a simple random walk needs to visit every vertex. Classical analysis gives bounds in terms of the number of vertices $n$ and edges $m$: Aleliunas et al.\\ showed that the cover time from any start is at most $2m(n-1)$, and Feige proved that no connected graph exceeds $(4/27+o(1))n^3$ and none falls below $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$ \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. These statements are tight for extremal graphs, but they say little about what to expect for a particular small graph: the bound $2m(n-1)$ is attained up to constant factors only by bottlenecked graphs such as paths and lollipops, and on dense or well-connected graphs it overshoots by a factor that grows with $n$ (Section~\\ref{sec:rationale}).\n60:\\paragraph{Worst-case bounds.} The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al.\\ follows by summing commute times, each at most $2m$, over the $n-1$ edges of a spanning tree. Feige's upper bound, $(4/27+o(1))n^3$, is attained by lollipop-like graphs, and his companion lower bound, $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, is attained by the complete graph \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. Together these delimit the possible range of cover times for a given $n$ but are worst-case over all connected graphs; they are not intended as point predictions for a given graph. Lov\\'asz's survey on random walks on graphs collects the hitting-time and commute-time machinery behind such bounds. Our comparison against $2m(n-1)$ therefore measures how much a worst-case guarantee costs as a predictor on small graphs; it is not a criticism of the bound, which remains valid.\n62:\\paragraph{Cover times of specific models.} A complementary line of work derives the cover and hitting times of particular random-graph models, for example hyperbolic random graphs \\citep{Kiwi2022CoverAH}. Such results exploit the generative structure of one model and give asymptotic statements. Our setting is different in three ways: the graphs form a mixture of twelve deterministic and random families, they are small, and the predictor is not told the family, so it must rely on statistics that are cheap to compute for any input graph.\n199:\\paragraph{Range of validity.} All graphs have $n\\le 30$ and are drawn from twelve named families; labels are start-averaged, not worst-case-start, cover times. Extrapolation to larger graphs, to other families (e.g.\\ scale-free or hyperbolic random graphs \\citep{Kiwi2022CoverAH}) or to the maximum over start vertices is untested. A power-law fit calibrated at $n\\le 30$ has no guarantee at larger $n$ and, in particular, should not be expected to respect the extremal exponents known from \\citet{Feige1995ATU}.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bd783da-1739-49a5-a1ff-6fbbac76c044","uuid":"fdd0a8ed-da9a-476b-9059-53e5b7c80015","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:49:01.833Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"[paper.sh] reference query plan ready: 5 queries\n[semantic_scholar] search start | query 1/5 | Random walks, universal traversal se