{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015qiiSD12v7q7JXcXUhXLcs","type":"tool_result","content":"dict_keys(['count', 'generated_at', 'queries', 'references'])\n{\"citation_count\": 725, \"citation_key\": \"Aleliunas1979RandomWU\", \"id\": \"ref.s2.4dcf27fa98e8d36a29270b0e100ebe46d74a27ce\", \"paper_id\": \"4dcf27fa98e8d36a29270b0e100ebe46d74a27ce\", \"query\": \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\", \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-10-27T17:49:18.207362+00:00\", \"source_url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/4dcf27fa98e8d36a29270b0e100ebe46d74a27ce\", \"title\": \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\", \"venue\": \"20th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (sfcs 1979)\", \"year\": 1979}\n{\"citation_count\": 761, \"citation_key\": \"Lov\\u00e1sz2001RandomWO\", \"id\": \"ref.s2.99ae64fdd0e1cf6ede9b4f4a18a4323fd05f29d1\", \"paper_id\": \"99ae64fdd0e1cf6ede9b4f4a18a4323fd05f29d1\", \"query\": \"Lov\\u00e1sz Random walks on graphs: a survey\", \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-10-27T17:49:21.371040+00:00\", \"source_url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/99ae64fdd0e1cf6ede9b4f4a18a4323fd05f29d1\", \"title\": \"Random Walks on Graphs: A Survey\", \"venue\": \"\", \"year\": 2001}\n{\"citation_count\": 316, \"citation_key\": \"Doyle1984RandomWA\", \"id\": \"ref.s2.e6a31159b80292d82ef3ed696df9336677cf8534\", \"paper_id\": \"e6a31159b80292d82ef3ed696df9336677cf8534\", \"query\": \"Lov\\u00e1sz Random walks on graphs: a survey\", \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-10-27T17:49:21.874104+00:00\", \"source_url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/e6a31159b80292d82ef3ed696df9336677cf8534\", \"title\": \"Random Walks and Electrical Networks\", \"venue\": \"\", \"year\": 1984}\n35:Worst-case bounds on the cover time of a simple random walk, such as $2m(n-1)$, are known to be loose for concrete small graphs. We ask whether five cheap, relabeling-invariant statistics (vertices, edges, mean degree, maximum degree, diameter) are enough t\n39:The cover time of a connected undirected graph is the expected number of steps a simple random walk needs to visit every vertex. Classical analysis gives bounds in terms of the number of vertices $n$ and edges $m$: Aleliunas et al.\\ showed that the cover ti\n43:\\paragraph{What is studied.} The object is a deliberately simple reference predictor: standardised log-statistics, ridge regression, and a power-law reading of the result, $\\widehat{C}\\propto \\prod_j s_j^{w_j}$. We claim no novelty for the model class. The \n47:\\item A precisely specified five-statistic log-linear ridge predictor for start-averaged cover time on connected graphs with $6\\le n\\le 30$, with an observation that three of its five features are exactly collinear, so the ridge penalty is structurally nece\n55:\\caption{Overview of the study. A connected graph ($6\\le n\\le 30$) is reduced to five relabeling-invariant statistics; a ridge model predicts log cover time, which is compared with a Monte Carlo label. The $2m(n-1)$ bound, a constant predictor and a shuffle\n60:\\paragraph{Worst-case bounds.} The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al.\\ follows by summing commute times, each at most $2m$, over the $n-1$ edges of a spanning tree. Feige's upper bound, $(4/27+o(1))n^3$, is attained by lollipop-like graphs, and his compani\n89:\\paragraph{Five-statistic ridge predictor (studied).} For a graph with maximum degree $\\Delta$, diameter $D$ (at least $1$) and mean degree $\\bar d=2m/n$, the feature vector is\n97:with the intercept $\\bar y$ unpenalised, and the prediction is $\\hat y(G)=\\exp\\!\\big(\\bar y+z(G)^\\top w\\big)$ for the standardised feature $z(G)$. Because the model is linear in log-features it is a power law, $\\hat y\\propto n^{a_1}m^{a_2}\\bar d^{a_3}\\Delta\n101:\\paragraph{Constant baseline.} The prediction is the geometric mean of the training Monte Carlo targets, $\\exp(\\bar y)$, for every test graph. This is exactly the ridge model with all weights set to zero, so the ridge-versus-constant comparison is a contro\n103:\\paragraph{Shuffled-target control.} The ridge predictions are scored against test targets permuted across graphs by a fixed derangement. A predictor that tracked the targets because of an artefact of the metric, rather than of graph structure, would score\n107:\\paragraph{Effective parameterisation.} Because $\\ln m=\\ln n+\\ln\\bar d-\\ln 2$, the five-feature power law is equivalent to a four-parameter family in $(\\ln n,\\ln \\bar d,\\ln\\Delta,\\ln D)$; the ridge solution distributes the shared signal over the three coll\n125:The predictor is a self-contained Python module of about $140$ lines implementing the reset/fit/predict interface using only the standard library: breadth-first search from every vertex gives the diameter; a small Gaussian elimination with partial pivoting\n131:Table~\\ref{tab:main} and Figure~\\ref{fig:main} report the sealed-evaluation scores. The five-statistic ridge predictor attains a family-macro log MAE of $0.181$ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.macro_log_mae]]\n136:so the ridge error is about one fifth of the constant error and about one tenth of the bound error (ratios $4.8$ and $9.7$; arithmetic on the reported values).\n141:\\caption{Family-macro log MAE on the sealed held-out graphs (lower is better). The ridge predictor is compared with the $2m(n-1)$ bound, a constant predictor equal to the training geometric mean, and the ridge predictions scored against shuffled targets. E\n147:\\caption{Sealed-evaluation results. ``Factor'' is $\\exp$ of the family-macro log MAE and ``Ratio'' is the log MAE divided by that of the ridge predictor; both are arithmetic conversions of the reported values.}\n154:Five-statistic ridge & $0.181$ & $1.20$ & $1.0$ \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.macro_log_mae]]\n156:Ridge scored vs.\\ shuffled targets & $1.423$ & $4.15$ & $7.8$ \\\\ % @evidence[[paper_data.json#final_evaluation.metrics.shuffle_macro_log_mae]]\n163:\\paragraph{Constant control as feature ablation.} Because the constant predictor shares the ridge intercept, the drop from $0.870$ to $0.181$ is attributable, within this evaluation, to the five-statistic term as a whole. The evaluation does \\emph{not} con\n165:\\paragraph{Shuffled-target control.} Scoring the same predictions against permuted targets yields $1.423$, much larger than $0.181$ and comparable in magnitude to the bound's $1.754$. The low error of the ridge predictor is therefore not a property of the \n172:about $2.9\\%$, or roughly one sixth of the ridge log MAE ($0.029/0.181\\approx0.16$; arithmetic). The label noise is thus small relative to the prediction error and very small relative to the bound's error, and the headline gap cannot be explained by label \n173:This is an order-of-magnitude statement under a Gaussian approximation, not a measured noise floor, but it indicates that label noise explains only a minor part of the ridge error. Two cautions apply. First, this is a median: the retained record does not i\n195:\\paragraph{Training-set size.} The model is fitted on $36$ labelled graphs (three per family) with five features of rank four; the ridge penalty $\\lambda=1$ is a fixed constant. Whether the result is sensitive to $\\lambda$, to training-set size or to the c\n197:\\paragraph{Feature attribution and ceiling.} We do not know which features carry the gain, nor how close a function of these five statistics can get to the label: structurally different graphs can share all five statistics. Richer invariants (for instance,\n214:Ridge predictor has lower family-macro log MAE than the $2m(n-1)$ bound on the sealed graphs & Supported ($0.181$ vs.\\ $1.754$) \\\\\n227:For connected graphs with at most $30$ vertices, a ridge regression on five relabeling-invariant log-statistics predicted Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time with a family-macro log MAE of $0.181$ in a sealed evaluation, compared with $1.754$ for the gen\n244:\\caption{Implementation elements of the five-statistic ridge predictor.}\n252:Ridge penalty & $\\lambda=1$ on standardised features; intercept unpenalised & 49, 106--118 \\\\\n308:The configuration studied here is the reference configuration retained by model selection. The selection procedure considered one alternative candidate, a resistance-calibrated Matthews-style predictor with a ridge residual, and it produced no score: zero","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bd783da-1739-49a5-a1ff-6fbbac76c044","uuid":"dac4555c-90e9-4f80-a224-893f5881f222","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:49:53.459Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"dict_keys(['count', 'generated_at', 'queries', 'references'])\n{\"citation_count\": 725, \"citation_key\": \"Aleliunas1979RandomWU\", \"id\": \"ref.s2.4dcf27fa98e8d36a29270b0e100ebe46d74a27ce\", \"paper_id\": \"4dcf27fa98e8d36a29270b0e100ebe46d74a27ce\", \"query\": \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\", \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-10-27T17:49:18.207362+00:00\", \"source_url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/4dcf27fa98e8d36a29270b0e100ebe46d74a27ce\", \"title\": \"Random walks, universal traversal sequences, and the complexity of maze problems\", \"venue\": \"20th Annual Symposium on Foundations of Computer Science (sfcs 1979)\", \"year\": 1979}\n{\"citation_count\": 761, \"citation_key\": \"Lov\\u00e1sz2001RandomWO\", \"id\": \"ref.s2.99ae64fdd0e1cf6ede9b4f4a18a4323fd05f29d1\", \"paper_id\": \"99ae64fdd0e1cf6ede9b4f4a18a4323fd05f29d1\", \"query\": \"Lov\\u00e1sz Random walks on graphs: a survey\", \"retrieved_at\": \"2026-10-27T17:49:21.371040+00:00\", \"source_url\": \"https://api.semanticscholar.org/graph/v1/paper/99ae64fdd0e1cf6ede9b4f4a18a4323fd05f29d1\", \"title\": \"Random Walks on Graphs: A Survey\", \"venue\": \"\", \"year\": 2001}\n{\"citation_count\"