{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgTAVwdQ1BuTnhuTbyTP3","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_011vNHnUnxBT59sF4ynopGoK","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; python3 - <<'E'\nimport json\nnew=open('/tmp/new.bib').read().replace('Lovász2001RandomWO','Lovasz2001RandomWO')\nopen('references.bib','a').write('\\n'+new)\np=json.load(open('references_provenance.json'));n=json.load(open('/tmp/new.json'))\nfor r in n['references']:\n    if r['citation_key'].startswith('Lov'): r['citation_key']='Lovasz2001RandomWO'\n    p['references'].append(r)\np['count']=len(p['references'])\njson.dump(p,indent=1,fp=open('references_provenance.json','w'),ensure_ascii=False)\nE\ngrep -c \"^@\" references.bib; sed -n 39p paper.tex | cut -c1-200; sed -n 60p paper.tex; sed -n 197p paper.tex; sed -n 227,229p paper.tex; sed -n 308,312p paper.tex; sed -n 85,88p paper.tex; grep -n \"Predicting a graph\\|Statistical prediction\" paper.tex"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4006,"cache_read_input_tokens":48432,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4006,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bd783da-1739-49a5-a1ff-6fbbac76c044","uuid":"55ab5734-961a-4a53-99f1-1c1a6db97c21","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:49:57.540Z","request_id":"req_011CgTAVvjpZJk5zxRvTY36L"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011vNHnUnxBT59sF4ynopGoK","type":"tool_result","content":"6\nThe cover time of a connected undirected graph is the expected number of steps a simple random walk needs to visit every vertex. Classical analysis gives bounds in terms of the number of vertices $n$ \n\\paragraph{Worst-case bounds.} The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al.\\ follows by summing commute times, each at most $2m$, over the $n-1$ edges of a spanning tree. Feige's upper bound, $(4/27+o(1))n^3$, is attained by lollipop-like graphs, and his companion lower bound, $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, is attained by the complete graph \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. Together these delimit the possible range of cover times for a given $n$ but are worst-case over all connected graphs; they are not intended as point predictions for a given graph. Lov\\'asz's survey on random walks on graphs collects the hitting-time and commute-time machinery behind such bounds. Our comparison against $2m(n-1)$ therefore measures how much a worst-case guarantee costs as a predictor on small graphs; it is not a criticism of the bound, which remains valid.\n\\paragraph{Feature attribution and ceiling.} We do not know which features carry the gain, nor how close a function of these five statistics can get to the label: structurally different graphs can share all five statistics. Richer invariants (for instance, resistance-based quantities motivated by the commute-time identity and Matthews-type bounds) are natural candidates, but a configuration of that kind produced no score in this study and we draw no conclusion about it.\nFor connected graphs with at most $30$ vertices, a ridge regression on five relabeling-invariant log-statistics predicted Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time with a family-macro log MAE of $0.181$ in a sealed evaluation, compared with $1.754$ for the generic $2m(n-1)$ bound, $0.870$ for a constant predictor, and $1.423$ when the predictions were scored against shuffled targets. Median relative Monte Carlo label error was $0.029$, small compared with the gap. The evaluation completed with a qualified outcome. In answer to the first research question, cheap statistics can give a markedly more accurate point prediction than the bound on this sample; in answer to the second, the retained evidence does not say on which graph types the predictor is reliable or fails. The most valuable next measurements are inexpensive and well-defined: per-family and per-size error reporting with Monte Carlo and graph-bootstrap uncertainty, per-feature ablations, sensitivity to the ridge penalty and training-set composition, and a comparison with richer invariants such as resistance-based features.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\nThe configuration studied here is the reference configuration retained by model selection. The selection procedure considered one alternative candidate, a resistance-calibrated Matthews-style predictor with a ridge residual, and it produced no score: zero candidates were scored and one was unscored. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\nUnscored candidates are missing evidence rather than measured losses, and we draw no conclusion about that alternative. The selection score of the retained configuration was $-0.1913$ (a negated macro log MAE on the selection test graphs), identical to the reference by construction, so the score difference is $0.0$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.baseline_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.search_score_delta]]\nSelection scores and sealed-evaluation scores come from different graph sets and different labels, and are not comparable; in particular, $0.191$ versus $0.181$ must not be read as an improvement or a degradation.\n\n\\subsection{Reproduction notes}\n\\label{fig:gap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Compared predictors}\n64:\\paragraph{Statistical prediction with cheap features.} Predicting a graph functional from low-dimensional summaries is a standard use of regression. What distinguishes this task is that the label is itself a Monte Carlo estimate with its own standard error, that the error is naturally multiplicative (cover times differ by orders of magnitude across graphs of similar size), and that the family composition of the test set drives the aggregate. We therefore work in log-space and aggregate over families rather than over graphs, so that a family with many graphs cannot dominate the score.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0bd783da-1739-49a5-a1ff-6fbbac76c044","uuid":"d6c39280-e841-467f-bb7b-6d79e68810b9","timestamp":"2026-10-27T17:49:57.704Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"6\nThe cover time of a connected undirected graph is the expected number of steps a simple random walk needs to visit every vertex. Classical analysis gives bounds in terms of the number of vertices $n$ \n\\paragraph{Worst-case bounds.} The $2m(n-1)$ bound of Aleliunas et al.\\ follows by summing commute times, each at most $2m$, over the $n-1$ edges of a spanning tree. Feige's upper bound, $(4/27+o(1))n^3$, is attained by lollipop-like graphs, and his companion lower bound, $(1+o(1))\\,n\\ln n$, is attained by the complete graph \\citep{Feige1995ATU,Feige1995ATL}. Together these delimit the possible range of cover times for a given $n$ but are worst-case over all connected graphs; they are not intended as point predictions for a given graph. Lov\\'asz's survey on random walks on graphs collects the hitting-time and commute-time machinery behind such bounds. Our comparison against $2m(n-1)$ therefore measures how much a worst-case guarantee costs as a predictor on small graphs; it is not a criticism of the bound, which remains valid.\n\\paragraph{Feature attribution and ceiling.} We do not know which features carry the gain, nor how close a function of these five statistics can get to the label: structurally different graphs can share all five statistics. Richer invariants (for instance, resistance-based quantities motivated by the commute-time identity and Matthews-type bounds) are natural candidates, but a configuration of that kind produced no score in this study and we draw no conclusion about it.\nFor connected graphs with at most $30$ vertices, a ridge regression on five relabeling-invariant log-statistics predicted Monte Carlo start-averaged cover time with a family-macro log MAE of $0.181$ in a sealed evaluation, compared with $1.754$ for the generic $2m(n-1)$ bound, $0.870$ for a constant predictor, and $1.423$ when the predictions were scored against shuffled targets. Median relative Monte Carlo label error was $0.029$, small compared with the gap. The evaluation completed with a qualified outcome. In answer to the first research question, cheap statistics can give a markedly more accurate point prediction than the bound on this sample; in answer to the second, the retained evidence does not say on which graph types the predictor is reliable or fails. The most valuable next measurements are inexpensive and well-defined: per-family and per-size error reporting with Monte Carlo and graph-bootstrap uncertainty, per-feature ablations, sensitivity to the ridge penalty and training-set composition, and a comparison with richer invariants such as resistance-based features.\n\n% IMPORTANT: \\clearpage flushes all pending floats before references so that\nThe configuration studied here is the reference configuration retained by model selection. The selection procedure considered one alternative candidate, a resistance-calibrated Matthews-style predictor with a ridge residual, and it produced no score: zero candidates were scored and one was unscored. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.attempt_counts]]\nUnscored candidates are missing evidence rather than measured losses, and we draw no conclusion about that alternative. The selection score of the retained configuration was $-0.1913$ (a negated macro log MAE on the selection test graphs), identical to the reference by construction, so the score difference is $0.0$. % @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.baseline_score]] @evidence[[paper_data.json#downstream_study.search_score_delta]]\nSelection scores and sealed-evaluation scores come from different graph sets and different labels, and are not comparable; in particular, $0.191$ versus $0.181$ must not be read as an improvement or a degradation.\n\n\\subsection{Reproduction notes}\n\\label{fig:gap}\n\\end{figure}\n\n\\subsection{Compared predictors}\n64:\\paragraph{Statistical prediction with cheap features.} Predicting a graph functional from low-dimensional summaries is a standard use of regression. What distinguishes this task is that the label is itself a Monte Carlo estimate with its own standard error, that the error is naturally multiplicative (cover times differ by orders of magnitude across graphs of similar size), and that the family composition of the test set drives the aggregate. We therefore work in log-space and aggregate over families rather than over graphs, so that a family with many graphs cannot dominate the score.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
